The `useState` hook returns a new state object with the values passed as arguments. When the component mounts, it calls `callback` with the current state object. Then, when the component unmounts, it updates the state using `setState`.

By using `useState`, you avoid polluting your global state with unnecessary components or methods. Instead, each component has its own local state, which makes your code more modular and easier to maintain.

